  "rule": {
    "title": "had better, would rather",
    "explanation": "«Had better» + verbo (sin «to») es un consejo fuerte o un aviso, para una situación concreta. Suele implicar que, si no, habrá un problema. La negativa es «had better not»:\n- «You'd better call Helen now.» (será mejor que la llames ya)\n- «We'd better not be late.»\n\nAunque «had» parece pasado, habla del presente o del futuro. Casi siempre se contrae: «you'd better», «we'd better».\n\n«Would rather» + verbo (sin «to») expresa preferencia:\n- «I'd rather wait until Monday.» (prefiero esperar)\n- «I'd rather work late than miss the deadline.»\n- «Would you rather call him or email him?»\n- «I'd rather not talk about it.»\n\nPara preferir que otra persona haga algo: «would rather» + sujeto + past simple:\n- «I'd rather you didn't tell anyone.» (preferiría que no se lo dijeras a nadie)\n\nOjo: las dos van con el verbo sin «to» (nunca «you'd better to call» ni «I'd rather to wait»), y la negativa va detrás: «had better not», «would rather not».",
    "table": {
      "headers": [
        "Forma",
        "Uso",
        "Ejemplo"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "had better + verbo",
          "consejo fuerte, aviso",
          "You'd better hurry."
        ],
        [
          "had better not + verbo",
          "aviso negativo",
          "We'd better not be late."
        ],
        [
          "would rather + verbo",
          "preferencia",
          "I'd rather stay in."
        ],
        [
          "would rather… than…",
          "comparar",
          "I'd rather walk than drive."
        ],
        [
          "would rather + sujeto + pasado",
          "preferir que otro…",
          "I'd rather you stayed."
        ]
      ]
    }
  },
